## PART II: AXIOMS

### A1. Axiom of Logical Plurality (ALP)
There exists more than one consistent logical system.

> **Formal:**
> ∃L₁ ∃L₂ (L₁ ≠ L₂ ∧ Consistent(L₁) ∧ Consistent(L₂))

*Justification:* Intuitionistic logic, classical logic, paraconsistent logic, relevance logic, fuzzy logic, linear logic, and modal logic all exist as consistent formal systems.

---

### A2. Axiom of Axiomatic Choice (AAC)
For every formal system, its axioms are chosen, not necessitated.

> **Formal:**
> ∀FS ∃Agent (Agent chose Axioms(FS))

*Justification:* Axioms are not discovered in nature; they are selected by logicians for specific purposes. No axiom is forced upon us.

---

### A3. Axiom of Rule Indeterminacy (ARI)
Rules of inference cannot justify themselves without circularity.

> **Formal:**
> ¬∃R (R ⊢ Justification(R))

*Justification:* Any attempt to justify Modus Ponens using Modus Ponens is circular. Any attempt to justify it otherwise requires a prior system.

---

### A4. Axiom of Historical Emergence (AHE)
Logic has changed over time and continues to change.

> **Formal:**
> ∃t₁ ∃t₂ (L(t₁) ≠ L(t₂))

*Justification:* Aristotelian logic differs from Fregean logic. Classical logic differs from intuitionistic logic. Logic is historically situated.

---

### A5. Axiom of Extra-Logical Selection (AES)
The choice between competing logical systems is not determined by logic itself.

> **Formal:**
> ∀L₁ ∀L₂ (L₁ ≠ L₂ → ¬∃L (L ⊢ Selection(L₁, L₂)))

*Justification:* Logic provides no criterion for choosing between classical and intuitionistic logic. Both are consistent. The choice is extra-logical.

---

### A6. Axiom of Normative Encoding (ANE)
Every formal system embodies values and preferences.

> **Formal:**
> ∀FS ∃V (V ∈ Values(FS))

*Justification:* The choice of bivalence over multivalence, consistency over paraconsistency, excluded middle over openness—these are preferences for certain values over others.

---

### A7. Axiom of Semantic Incompleteness (ASI)
Meaning is not fully captured by syntax.

> **Formal:**
> ∀FS ∃M (M is meaningful ∧ M ∉ Syntax(FS))

*Justification:* Tarski's distinction between object language and meta-language demonstrates that meaning exceeds formal expression.

---

### A8. Axiom of Foundation Failure (AFF)
No formal system can ground itself without circularity or infinite regress.

> **Formal:**
> ¬∃FS (FS ⊢ Ground(FS))

*Justification:* Gödel's incompleteness theorems demonstrate that formal systems cannot prove their own consistency. This is a special case of the general principle.

---

### A9. Axiom of Contingent Necessity (ACN)
Statements presented as "logically necessary" are actually contingent on chosen axioms.

> **Formal:**
> ∀P (P is "logically necessary" in L → P is contingent on Axioms(L))

*Justification:* LEM is necessary in classical logic but not in intuitionistic logic. PNC is necessary in classical logic but not in paraconsistent logic. What is "necessary" depends on the system.

---

### A10. Axiom of Choice Disclosure (ACD)
When a system cannot justify its own foundations, those foundations must be understood as chosen.

> **Formal:**
> (¬∃S (S ⊢ Justification(S))) → Chosen(S)

*Justification:* If a foundation cannot be proven, and yet it is accepted, the acceptance must be an act of choice.

